          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows a loving emotional attitude toward St John, explicitly qualifying it as sisterly rather than romantic or marital love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The avowal explains why Jane had been willing to venture abroad with St John; it does not itself promise that she will go or affirm that she is bound to do so. In the same turn she expressly denies any obligation or breach of promise. Her separate offer to serve as his assistant is disputed elsewhere in the scene and should not be transferred to the focal expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a reasoned rebuttal and functions as a deliberate explanation of Jane's attitude and earlier willingness, not as a reflex-like affective outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I replied—\\n\\n“There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression's principal force is adequately captured as an avowal of specifically sisterly affection. Its explanatory and conciliatory role in the argument does not constitute a distinct core mode beyond T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During a dispute about whether Jane has promised or is obliged to undertake missionary work in India, Jane rejects St John's accusation of breach or desertion. She says that her admiration, trust, and sisterly love explain why she would have ventured much with him, while maintaining that she is not obligated to go and cannot accept the expected consequences.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal avowal creates no undertaking. Jane separately had offered to accompany St John as an assistant rather than as his wife, but he rejected that arrangement and proposed that she assist another missionary. In the focal turn she expressly denies any promise or obligation. St John responds by challenging her fear for her life, not by accepting the love avowal as a commitment.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification is based only on the supplied blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports sincere sisterly affection through Jane's direct avowal and surrounding statements of admiration, confidence, and friendship. It does not establish every aspect of her long-term intentions toward St John, and the later supplied conclusion does not convert this occurrence into a promise or marital commitment.\"}"
